one of the biggest  challenges during the pandemic has been  keeping an eye  on vulnerable children parents are  saying that they don't want the child at  home anymore they want him accommodated  we've been allowed inside britain's  biggest social work department  at kent county council dad has a bad  weekend and then he'll say i can't have  him anymore and then  things will settle and then they're in a  bit of a circle  when the phone rings here it means  there's an immediate concern for the  welfare of a child  people think that pr means parental  rights it doesn't it means parental  responsibility  your responsibility to your child and  people turn up here with their child  stuff in a black sack and just say i  don't want to  take them yeah and it's one of the  things i really struggle with as a  social worker  we carried out a national poll of social  workers  and nearly half 45 percent said the  number of children being put on child  protection plans is up compared to the  same period last year  and the majority 80 percent said they  didn't feel like they had enough  resources  to cope with the level of demand they  now face  but she's basically said that her mum  has been using drugs  um and there's sort of syringes in all  sorts around the house  she can't live there anymore and she  wants to go to college she wants to do  some positive things in her life she  just wants  somewhere safe to be all right bye  but the case that's just come in isn't  about a parent  who's had enough of their child it's  about a child  who's had enough of their parent mum's  got a real history of class a drug use  which has got clearly out of control so  here we have a youngster  phoning you guys about her mum that that  was  doesn't happen very often this is  extremely rare  it's the first time that i've certainly  heard of it in my current role  thank you for meeting me in the first  place social worker davina mcmohan  meets the 16 year old in a local park  layla  it's not her real name says during  lockdown her mum's drug use has spiraled  out of control  and she's had enough we've changed her  voice to protect her identity  what would you like to happen what do  you think would be  you'd be happy with that yeah okay  because you've been through that before  yeah and you feel comfortable for that  okay  um because you're 16 you can actually  give your own consent for that to happen  davina  now has to find emergency accommodation  for layla  oh hi sarah it's dee so she's basically  saying that she would she would like a  placement she's reporting that mum  has been using heroin before which is  obviously the concerns and why  they were removed when they're younger  she's been staying with her brother  he's saying that he can't keep her there  he's got two young children there's not  the surprise  leyla's story is one of a childhood  marred by her mother's addiction to  drugs  i thought i could help her so i tried  hanging on for so long  and i realized it just has to end but i  wanted so i tried so hard to just  try and get her through it and make sure  she was okay  and my worry was if i left her would she  go like to drugs harder than what she is  now  and in the end i just said i can't do it  anymore  this must have been a very difficult  decision for you to make  yeah this is your mom but she's done it  before and  i've been used to it  are you wanting that break now yeah  i feel i feel like um it will help as  well  so i can actually get my life back on  track go to college sort my education  out get a job  and just move on with my life without  her in it  because she's a setback to life with all  of us she's going to set back to life  layla is caught between a rock and a  hard place her home life  hectic but care is far from ideal  all the research tells us that outcomes  for children in care are not great  and so maintaining children within their  families and within their kind of social  network is absolutely key  i think one of t